        "original_id": "4bca5d1b-c992-55f6-a78a-152bfd6f6b48",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Dahlia, Aruncus, Butea monosperma, Coreopsis, Toxicodendron vernicifluum, Rhus verniciflua\n- Chemicals: Butein\n- Concepts: genera, herbaceous perennial plants, Rosaceae, antioxidative effects, aldose reductase inhibition, advanced glycation endproducts inhibition\n\nFacts:\n- Dahlia is a genus of bushy, tuberous, herbaceous perennial plants native to Mexico.\n- Aruncus is a genus of clump-forming herbaceous perennial plants in the family Rosaceae.\n- Butein can be found in Toxicodendron vernicifluum, formerly called Rhus verniciflua, as well as in Dahlia, Butea monosperma, and Coreopsis.\n- Butein has antioxidative effects and inhibits aldose reductase and advanced glycation endproducts.",
